Below is a small, self‑contained C++ program that shows the most common ways to use the two classic associative containers in the standard library:  

* **`std::map`** – a red‑black tree that keeps the keys sorted.  
* **`std::unordered_map`** – a hash table that offers average‑case constant‑time look‑ups but no ordering guarantee.

```cpp
/********************************************************************
 *  Example: Working with std::map and std::unordered_map
 *
 *  The program demonstrates:
 *      • Insertion (emplace, operator[])
 *      • Lookup with find()
 *      • Iteration (ordered vs. unordered)
 *      • Erasing elements
 *      • Using a custom comparator with std::map
 ********************************************************************/

#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     *  1. std::map – ordered associative container
     * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    std::map<std::string, int> wordCount;  // key: word, value: frequency

    // Insert using emplace (constructs in place, no copy)
    wordCount.emplace("apple", 3);

    // Insert / update using operator[]
    wordCount["banana"] = 5;
    wordCount["cherry"] = 2;

    // Increment the count for an existing key
    wordCount["apple"]++;

    // Check whether a key exists
    if (auto it = wordCount.find("banana"); it != wordCount.end()) {
        std::cout << "banana count: " << it->second << '\n';
    }

    // Iterate – keys come out in ascending order by default
    std::cout << "\nWord counts (ordered by key):\n";
    for (const auto& [word, count] : wordCount) {
        std::cout << word << " : " << count << '\n';
    }

    /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     *  2. std::unordered_map – hash‑based associative container
     * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    std::unordered_map<int, std::string> idToName;   // key: id, value: name

    // Insert using emplace and operator[]
    idToName.emplace(42, "Alice");
    idToName.emplace(7,  "Bob");
    idToName[13] = "Charlie";

    std::cout << "\nName with id 7: " << idToName[7] << '\n';

    // Iterate – order is unspecified (depends on hash buckets)
    std::cout << "\nID → Name mapping (unordered):\n";
    for (const auto& [id, name] : idToName) {
        std::cout << id << " -> " << name << '\n';
    }

    // Remove an element
    idToName.erase(42);

    std::cout << "\nAfter deletion of id 42:\n";
    for (const auto& [id, name] : idToName) {
        std::cout << id << " -> " << name << '\n';
    }

    /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     *  3. Using std::map with a custom comparator
     * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    // Comparator that sorts strings in descending order
    auto descending = [](const std::string& a, const std::string& b) {
        return a > b;
    };

    std::map<std::string, int, decltype(descending)> reverseMap(descending);
    reverseMap = wordCount;          // copy the original map

    std::cout << "\nWord counts (descending order):\n";
    for (const auto& [word, count] : reverseMap) {
        std::cout << word << " : " << count << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### What this example demonstrates

| Feature | `std::map` | `std::unordered_map` |
|---------|------------|----------------------|
| **Key ordering** | Sorted by key (`operator<`) | No ordering guarantee |
| **Typical complexity** | `O(log n)` for insert, find, erase | `O(1)` average‑case, `O(n)` worst‑case |
| **Use‑case** | When you need sorted iteration | When you only need fast lookup |
| **Custom comparator** | Possible (e.g., reverse order) | Not applicable (hash table) |

Feel free to copy the code into a file (e.g., `assoc_example.cpp`) and compile with a modern C++ compiler:

```bash
g++ -std=c++20 assoc_example.cpp -o assoc_example
./assoc_example
```

This will print the word counts in ascending order, then the same counts in descending order, followed by the unordered map’s content before and after deleting an element.
